  "body": "## What changed\n\nNew module `camcraft-geometry::sketch_chains` (issue #1861, part of the DXF chain #1452). One call:\n\n```rust\npub fn chain(primitives: &[LayeredPrimitive], options: ChainOptions) -> Result<SketchChains, ChainError>\n```\n\nIt takes the DXF reader's lines, arcs and circles (#1860, merged as #1937) in millimetres and returns `closed` loops, `open` chains and `rejected` primitives.\n\n- **Joining.** Curve ends within the join tolerance (default 0.01 mm, allowed 1e-5 to 10 mm) are joined, per layer. A wider gap is never bridged: the chain stays open and reports its two free ends. A bridged gap is reported as `max_gap`.\n- **Arc metadata survives.** A chain segment is the input primitive turned to the walk direction (`reversed`, start angle and sweep sign rewritten). Nothing is flattened or snapped.\n- **Stable `ChainId`.** Closed loops are walked counterclockwise from their lowest joint, open chains from their lower end. The id is the 128-bit FNV-1a hash of the layer name and the curves in that order, quantized to 1e-6 mm. It does not depend on draw order or direction.\n- **Outer or hole.** Depth is counted by an exact crossing-number test against the arcs. Even depth is `Outer`, odd is `Hole`, with the innermost container as `parent`. This is the even-odd rule of `geo2d::Region`.\n- **Named refusals, per chain.** `DuplicatePrimitive`, `DegenerateSegment`, `Branch { at, degree }`, `SelfCrossing { at }`, `ZeroArea`, `DuplicateChain`. Closed chains that cross or touch each other keep their geometry but get `NestingAmbiguous` instead of a role, and so does every loop inside one of them. `SketchChains::strict()` turns the first of these into an `Err`.\n- **Hard errors.** A bad tolerance, a non-finite, out-of-range or invalid primitive, and a drawing over the limits (coordinates below 1e7 mm, 2,000,000 primitives, 200,000,000 pair tests) return `Err`. No `unwrap`, `expect` or `panic!` in the module.\n\nNo new dependency, no file I/O, no ECS. No user-facing command is added, so there is no MCP tool in this PR; the import command and its tool are #1862.\n\n## Algorithms and sources (clean room)\n\n| Step | Source |\n|---|---|\n| End clustering on a grid | Bentley, Stanat and Williams, 1977 |\n| Clusters and components | Tarjan, union-find, 1975 |\n| Identity hash | FNV-1a, IETF draft-eastlake-fnv |\n| Candidate pairs for the crossing search | Sweep and prune: Cohen, Lin, Manocha and Ponamgi, 1995; axis choice from Ericson, 2005 |\n| Segment, line-circle and circle-circle contacts | O'Rourke, 1998; Schneider and Eberly, 2003 |\n| Containment | Crossing number: Shimrat, 1962; Haines, 1994 |\n| Loop area with arcs | Green's theorem |\n\nEach submodule carries its provenance line. No vendor code, file or output was consulted. Decisions, alternatives dropped and known limits are in `docs/decisions/1861-sketch-chaining.md`.\n\n## How tested\n\n`cargo xtask gate quick --base upstream/main` on the final head: green, 10 steps, 0 failed, 30.2 min (head 3c65e18a2). A geometry change makes quick run the batch plan, so that is every debug suite plus clippy.\n\nPinning tests, 18 in three new suites under `crates/geometry/tests/`:\n\n- `sketch_chains.rs`\n  - `unordered_lines_chain_into_one_counterclockwise_loop`\n  - `chain_id_ignores_draw_order_and_direction_but_not_layer_or_geometry` (pins the id value; I recomputed it independently outside Rust)\n  - `arc_metadata_survives_chaining`\n  - `gap_wider_than_tolerance_stays_open_and_is_reported`\n  - `open_chain_of_lines_and_arcs_starts_at_its_lower_end`\n  - `arcs_that_close_a_circle_and_tangent_fillets_are_clean_loops`\n  - `dxf_polyline_bulges_and_circles_chain_with_their_arcs` (through `dxf::parse`; same id as the loose primitives)\n- `sketch_chains_nesting.rs`\n  - `nested_rectangles_and_circles_classify_as_outer_and_hole` (compared with `geo2d::Region`)\n  - `containment_follows_the_arcs_not_their_chords`\n  - `shuffled_nests_of_mixed_shapes_keep_their_depth_and_parent` (200 seeded nests, rotated, shuffled, flipped)\n  - `long_loop_chains_in_bounded_time` (40,000-side loop; 50,000-segment tall strip that pins the sweep axis)\n- `sketch_chains_refusals.rs`\n  - `self_crossing_loop_is_refused_by_name`\n  - `branching_vertex_is_a_named_diagnostic`\n  - `degenerate_pieces_are_refused_by_name`\n  - `invalid_input_is_an_error_not_a_panic`\n  - `crossing_loops_get_no_outer_or_hole_reading`\n  - `dense_drawing_is_refused_within_the_work_limit`\n  - `random_grid_sketches_never_panic_and_do_not_depend_on_input_order` (400 seeded sketches; every primitive chained or rejected once; depth checked against flattened polygons; accepted loops pass `Region::new`)\n\n## What was not exercised\n\n- No real-world DXF file was chained; inputs are hand-built or generated, plus one DXF text through the reader.\n- Two curves that meet at a tangent cusp can read as `SelfCrossing` when rounding makes them cross away from the joint. Fillets and S-curves are pinned as clean; the cusp case is not.\n- Open chains are not tested for self-crossing, by decision.\n- Nothing calls `chain()` yet: the ECS entity, the project file and the import command are #1862.\n- Debug-build timing only; the release lanes were not run.\n\nProcess change: no\n\nChangelog: Sketch chaining in camcraft-geometry: DXF lines, arcs and circles join within a tolerance into closed loops and open chains with stable ids, arcs kept analytic, outer-versus-hole nesting, and named refusals for branches, self-crossing loops and degenerate pieces (#1861)\n\nEffort: max (not escalated in this run; raised from high by the earlier attempt)\n\nFork synced at upstream/main a277dd9d5 (branch cut from upstream main, not from the fork's main).\n\nFixes #1861\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n",
